WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.625
DALE MARKOWITZ: So
today we're building

00:00:01.625 --> 00:00:03.660
this sort of DIY pet
cam for tracking what

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.340
your pets do while you're gone.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.632
Whether you want to know
whether your dog's hanging out

00:00:07.632 --> 00:00:10.380
on the couch, or your chickens
are trespassing on the porch,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.480
or your cat's just doing
some casual software

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.168
development while
you're gone, we'll

00:00:14.168 --> 00:00:16.710
build an app that runs a machine
learning model on your phone

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.960
to detect events,
like dog on couch,

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.150
and then sends you a
notification on Slack.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.790
It'll also save snapshots of
those moments to the cloud.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.250
So let's get to it and build
an ML powered pet cam that

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:27.450
runs on your smartphone.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.726
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:33.452 --> 00:00:35.160
One of the cool things
about this project

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.618
is that we're going to do the
computer vision part entirely

00:00:37.618 --> 00:00:40.110
in a web app that can
run on a smartphone.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.082
It'll be privacy conscious, so
we'll only send a notification

00:00:43.082 --> 00:00:45.540
or save a picture to the cloud
when your pet does something

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:46.428
that you care about.

00:00:46.428 --> 00:00:47.970
Otherwise, your
video and your photos

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.905
will stay entirely
on your local device.

00:00:50.905 --> 00:00:53.280
I'm building this pet cam with
my colleague, Jason Mayes,

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.560
a developer advocate and
host of the YouTube show,

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.150
"Made with TensorFlow.js."

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:58.030
Hey, Jason.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:00:59.697
JASON MAYES: Thanks
for having me, Dale.

00:00:59.697 --> 00:01:01.380
You know, we've got
a ton of old devices

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.720
like smartphones just
sitting around doing

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:04.950
absolutely nothing.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.380
So I really wanted to think
about how we can give those

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.170
a new life in a way that
anyone can then replicate

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.785
and hopefully use to
do something useful.

00:01:12.785 --> 00:01:14.160
DALE MARKOWITZ:
So why a pet cam?

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.340
JASON MAYES: Yeah,
that's a great question.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.370
So essentially, I saw a
lot of people around me

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.160
these days buying
kittens and puppies.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.737
And I think it'd be quite
fun to kind of track those

00:01:22.737 --> 00:01:24.570
as they're growing up
to make sure we're not

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.528
being too naughty when
you're leaving the house

00:01:26.528 --> 00:01:27.510
when they're alone.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.550
And of course, you
can always extend this

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:31.380
to other objects in
the future too as well.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:32.520
DALE MARKOWITZ: Kind
of like a chicken.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.270
JASON MAYES: Yeah,
like a chicken as well.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:35.575
Anything could be possible.

00:01:35.575 --> 00:01:37.200
DALE MARKOWITZ: All
right, let's do it.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.660
OK, so our app's going
to work like this.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.520
First, we'll build a
pet tracking camera

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.145
app that runs on your
phone and detects

00:01:43.145 --> 00:01:45.180
common pets like cats and dogs.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.310
Then we'll make our app
tracker even more powerful

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.890
by training a custom pet
model on Google Cloud

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:51.090
to detect chickens.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:51.660
[CLUCK]

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.340
And finally, we'll
connect our front end

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.960
to a back-end
built-in Firebase so

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.750
that we can store data and
send messages to Slack.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.280
JASON MAYES: OK,
so let's talk about

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.790
the actual front-end
application that tracks animals.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.400
The first step is to
enable the webcam access

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.940
and load a TensorFlow.js
model for object detection.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.460
Today we'll use
the COCO-SSD model

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.560
that recognizes 80
common household

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.410
objects in any picture
that you pass through it.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.380
Now, importing TensorFlow.js
and the machine learning model

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:21.490
is actually really simple.

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:24.510
First, we've got two script
tag imports for TensorFlow.js

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:25.660
and the model itself.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:27.870
Next, we can grab a
reference to the video object

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.850
that we're rendering
our webcam to.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:33.000
And once we've got that,
we can call COCO-SSD.load.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.650
We can then call model.detect
and pass it the video elements

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:40.650
on our web page to classify
the current frame being

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:41.970
played from our webcam.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.760
So once we can detect what's
in a given video frame,

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.860
we then need to send an alert
when the pet, in this example

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.590
a dog, gets close to an
object that we care about,

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:53.580
such as a food bowl,
couch, or a bed.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:55.620
Here, we simply allow
the user to choose

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.960
from the objects a model
can recognize to make

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.740
their selection as shown.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.460
And we ask the user to
ensure the device is set up

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.060
in front of the object we
want to monitor once enabled.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.360
Then, when we've detected that
the user has placed the device

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.820
in view of the object
they want to monitor,

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.980
we can continuously
check the video stream

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.490
to see if a chosen pet ever
gets close to that object.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.500
To do that, we
calculated the distance

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.210
between their bounding boxes.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.610
If a distance is less
than some threshold,

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.380
we'll send an alert
to our back end

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:26.970
to then send a notification.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:28.620
Now, we also had
to add some logic

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.590
to avoid spamming the user with
too many notifications in case

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:34.300
the dog came in and out
of frame continuously,

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.780
which you can learn all
about over in our blog post.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.700
So check the link in
the description after.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.370
And that's essentially
how the front end works.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.770
If you want to play with
yourself and grab the code,

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:45.845
you can find it over
on Glitch.com as shown.

00:03:45.845 --> 00:03:47.970
DALE MARKOWITZ: So after
Jason built the front end,

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:49.215
we can start testing the app.

00:03:49.215 --> 00:03:51.090
But the problem was that
Jason and I actually

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.520
don't own any pets, so we
had to get kind of creative

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.245
with our testing setups.

00:03:55.245 --> 00:03:57.870
JASON MAYES: And I also borrowed
Benjy the corgie from a friend

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:00.330
to check couch and
ball alerts for dogs,

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:02.880
which caught him every
single time, as you can see.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:04.463
It actually went
better than expected.

00:04:04.463 --> 00:04:06.088
DALE MARKOWITZ: But
then, in the middle

00:04:06.088 --> 00:04:07.740
of working on this
project, I actually

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:09.420
did acquire some pets--

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.160
two baby chicks.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.590
But despite how incredibly
adorable they were,

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:16.660
our pre-built TensorFlow model
didn't really recognize them,

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:18.600
so I had to figure out
a way to adopt pet cam

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.459
to work for my chicks.

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:23.190
Which brings us to part two,
training a custom chicken

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:24.900
detection model.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.480
I did this using Vertex
AI, Google's new machine

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.173
learning development platform.

00:04:29.173 --> 00:04:31.830
Look at these cute
little data points.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.980
AutoML Vision is a tool
within the Vertex AI platform

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.500
that lets you build a vision
model without writing any code

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.600
and then export it
to TensorFlow.js.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.550
First, in the
Vertex AI console, I

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:46.440
created a new image
object detection data set.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.428
Then I uploaded about 100
pictures of my new baby chicks

00:04:49.428 --> 00:04:50.970
to Google Cloud
Storage so they could

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:54.390
be imported into Vertex AI.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:56.670
After the data imported,
I opened an image

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:58.650
and annotated it by hand.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:01.153
I decided to train the model
to recognize Moira and Millie

00:05:01.153 --> 00:05:03.570
separately because they're two
different breeds of chicken

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:05.862
and I thought it would be
super cool if the model could

00:05:05.862 --> 00:05:06.720
tell them apart.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.000
I also annotated the position
of their food and water

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.125
bowl so that I could
calculate when the chicks were

00:05:11.125 --> 00:05:12.270
eating and drinking.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:13.890
Then I just did
this 100 more times,

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:16.860
and it took about 30 minutes.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.140
When I was done labeling,
I hit Train New Model

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:22.110
and then selected AutoML Edge
to create a model optimized

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:23.490
for an Edge device.

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:28.110
I named my model, selected
some configuration options,

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:31.800
configured it to train for five
node hours, and then waited.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:37.590
When my model was done training,
I checked out the precision

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:39.870
and recall in the Evaluate
tab, and this model

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:41.250
was pretty accurate.

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:44.100
To actually use it in my app,
go to the Deploy and Test tab,

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:45.990
click TensorFlow.js,
and then choose

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.253
a location in cloud storage
to export the model.

00:05:48.253 --> 00:05:49.920
From here, we can
download it and use it

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.260
right in our app's
JavaScript front end.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.030
All right, now let's
connect the back end.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:55.930
To make this app
actually useful,

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:58.000
we want to save these pet
events when they occur

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.420
and then send an alert
to the user over Slack.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:02.880
For this, I of course turned
to my favorite serverless tool

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:04.135
kit, Firebase.

00:06:04.135 --> 00:06:06.510
When the front end app detects
that a dog's on the couch,

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:08.820
it saves a snapshot and some
metadata about the event

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:09.660
to Firestore.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:11.490
Then I built a little
app, a front end,

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:13.620
to pull data from that
Firestore database

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:15.415
and then display it
as a sort of diary

00:06:15.415 --> 00:06:17.790
so that you can keep track of
all the cute or mischievous

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:20.460
things that your pet is
doing while you're gone.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:23.430
To set up Slack notifications,
I use a Firebase Cloud Function

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:25.075
to call the Slack API.

00:06:25.075 --> 00:06:26.700
It's set up with a
trigger so that when

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:28.620
data is written to the
Firestore database,

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:31.260
it automatically triggers
my Firebase Cloud Function

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:33.150
to run and then send an alert.

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:33.900
And that's it.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:34.942
That's the whole backend.

00:06:34.942 --> 00:06:37.150
If you want to learn how to
build a pet cam yourself,

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:39.360
check out the code in the
blog post linked below.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:40.290
JASON MAYES: You
can also check out

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:42.150
TensorFlow.js and
other premade models

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:43.980
you can use on our websites.

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:46.120
And of course, if you
want more inspiration,

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:48.983
check out #MadeWithTFJS hashtags
on Twitter and LinkedIn.

00:06:48.983 --> 00:06:50.400
DALE MARKOWITZ:
By the way, if you

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.108
want to see how my
colleague [? Marcue ?]

00:06:52.108 --> 00:06:54.360
built his very own pet
cam that runs on device

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.670
as well using Coral,
then check out his take

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:58.920
on a very similar project here.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:01.090
I guess great minds just
think alike or something.

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:02.310
And if you do try
this yourself, make

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:03.852
sure you let us know
in the comments.

00:07:03.852 --> 00:07:04.800
See you next time.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:07.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

